WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.810
Local LLM workloads vary widely.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.760
Interactive chat is latency-sensitive because a person is waiting.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.715
Coding agents may issue many sequential calls while reading files, proposing edits,

00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:10.450
and retrying.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.205
Batch summarization can tolerate longer waits but may create bursts.

00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:16.280
Document analysis stresses context length.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.480
Embedding jobs may use different models and endpoints entirely.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.080
Short requests and long-context requests create different routing needs.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.880
A quick prompt may finish in seconds.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.190
A large document prompt may spend significant time in prompt processing before generation

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:29.720
begins.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.680
If those requests are treated as identical,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.200
one backend can become clogged with long jobs while another could have served short

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.355
interactive work quickly.

00:00:36.355 --> 00:00:39.920
Concurrency turns simple user behavior into real capacity pressure.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.520
Several people may each think they are making one request,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.925
while the service sees overlapping prompt processing, token generation,

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:47.285
and streaming connections.

00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:51.120
A coding assistant or tool-using agent may keep a backend busy for minutes.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.160
Routing should consider how much work each server can accept before queueing or rejecting

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.155
new requests.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.185
Session continuity and retries also matter.

00:00:58.185 --> 00:01:02.297
Stateless clients send full context with each request and can route to any compatible

00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:02.866
backend.

00:01:02.866 --> 00:01:05.980
Other workflows rely on cache behavior, temporary state,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.285
or tool context and may need sticky sessions.

00:01:08.285 --> 00:01:11.380
Retries can improve reliability after a backend failure,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.560
but blind retries are risky when a request triggered actions or external tool use.
